      "abstract": "Dynamic power cables for floating offshore wind require accurate local curvature without prohibitive global analysis cost. This paper develops CableDyn, an implicit finite-element model that combines a continuously curved finite-bending cable element with a separate tension-only element for mooring lines. Static continuation establishes buoyant and contacting equilibria, generalised-alpha integration advances the dynamics, and continuous recovery locates extrema between nodes. Analytical solutions, systematic refinement, OrcaFlex and MoorDyn comparisons, and two independent model-scale experiments assess the model. In the Holcombe lazy-wave experiment, the marker-position error lies between about one and two percent of suspended length for the limiting end restraints, and the peak marker-scale curvature is recovered within 0.34 to 5.54 percent. The Bergdahl campaign validates dynamic upper-end force over thirty excitation conditions and two measured histories. Reference cables at three water depths, driven by prescribed platform motion, demonstrate moving touchdown, discrete-module effects, and three-dimensional wave and current response. Timings on the same workstation show performance comparable with OrcaFlex at shallow and intermediate depths and a threefold speed advantage in deep water. Implemented as a standalone program and an OpenFAST mooring module, CableDyn supplies mesh-converged tension, curvature, bend-moment and contact histories for global assessment and subsequent layered-section analysis.",
