    {
      "abstract": "Reliable prediction of mooring-line long-term extreme tension is essential for floating offshore wind turbine (FOWT) design, yet the full long-term analysis (FLTA) is computationally prohibitive. Furthermore, sparse environmental sampling obscures the critical sea-state domain and the physical mechanisms governing extreme mooring tensions. Machine-learning surrogates can accelerate the evaluation, but purely data-driven models cannot guarantee physically admissible predictions in the low-exceedance tail. To address these limitations, this study proposes an average conditional exceedance rate (ACER) based surrogate framework that integrates three physics-informed mechanisms: (i) the analytical ACER tail structure is embedded as a functional-domain constraint in the loss formulation; (ii) sequential wave-physics admissibility filters are applied to both observed and synthetic sea states; and (iii) differentiable aerodynamic descriptors encode the pitch-control regime transition. Consequently, the trained surrogate augments the FLTA integration by a factor of 30 in sea-state density at a fraction of the computational cost of direct simulation. Validation confirms high surrogate accuracy across the full wind-speed range, with prediction errors bounded within engineering-acceptable margins and no systematic bias across the aerodynamic regime transition. The surrogate-augmented FLTA yields 100-year return levels approximately 1.4% below the simulation-based baseline, because the 30-fold densification sharpens the wave-physics admissibility boundary that remains coarsely resolved under sparse sampling. The densified critical sea-state domain further reveals that the most consequential mooring loads originate from the moderate-wind, near-rated regime, where peak aerodynamic thrust acts before pitch regulation activates, rather than from extreme storm conditions. These findings confirm that the ACER-based surrogate can provide efficient, interpretable, and physically consistent prediction of long-term extreme mooring tension for FOWTs.",
